oh wow you guys aren't gonna believe how  delicious this is hi I'm Susan Feniger  today I'm gonna be making one of my  favorite dishes boyo Ciudad with pickled  tomatoes I think you should try this  dish because honestly even without the  chicken is one of my favorite dishes  I've ever made and that's for the last  37 years oh my gosh I'm old this is a  dish that I learned many many years ago  on one of my first trips to India and so  many of these flavors come from India  yet they're very similar to flavors we  use in the Latin kitchen from the time I  was a little girl I used to lick the  fudge paddles with my mom in the kitchen  and I started working in restaurants but  I didn't really think about it seriously  until I was in college and the army cook  I worked for was the one that said what  are you doing studying business used to  be a chef when I first started which is  way long ago I was one of the very few  women in French kitchens that I worked  in but you know what I was a tomboy and  so I was in there with all the guys and  I had a great time but for sure you had  to prove yourself so I think we probably  had to work harder as a woman than you  did as a man in those kitchens to prove  yourself but you know I think that made  me in some ways a better chef one of the  things I love about the restaurant  business I think for many LGBTQ youth  particularly who may have been rejected  from their family the restaurant  business can often create a second  family and support system that's  wonderful you know I've been out for so  many years as a lesbian in the  restaurant business and I think there's  so many people who are LGBTQ in the  restaurant business and always has been  and so for me this place was always very  safe and warm for me to be out and open  but for me I was always comfortable  being who I was in the kitchen  environment one of the things I want to  make sure you remember is when you're  adding in the cilantro stems and the  leaves don't cook it for very long  literally throw it in wilt it and turn  it off because what happens is as you  continue to cook it the leaves lose a  lot of their color and even some of  their freshness and flavor one of the  most important things while you're  cooking is taste all along the way I  like to taste every step to make sure  I've sees in each element properly so  you'll see that I'm adding part of the  heavy cream into the sauce initially  because sometimes my chilies will be  spicier than other times sometimes the  cilantro will have more flavor than  other times so if I added too much heavy  cream the sauce is too mellowed out you  could always add more but what you can't  do is take it out and you'd have to sort  of start from scratch again so add a  little bit and then add more later once  you taste it the flavors that we're  looking for with this sauce is I want to  have a hit of cumin a strong hint of  cilantro and then I want that vinegar  and the brown sugar balanced so you've  got the earthiness from the cilantro and  the cumin and the mushrooms and then  you've got the spice from the chilies  and you have the sweetness and acidity  from the vinegar brown sugar mixture all  those flavors together that to me gives  you this great strong flavor profile I'd  have to say the thing I probably love  the most about being in the restaurant  business is the creative aspect of it as  well as being in a position to be able  to give back to charities that are so  dear to me you know I have been on the  board of the Los Angeles LGBT Center for  the last 12 and a half years this is one  of the most rewarding things I have ever  done in my life and I'm honored to be a  part of it oh yes
 

